.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.TH GIT-CARVE-CLI 1 "2026-11-22" "git-carve 1.1.0" "Git Manual"
.SH NAME
git-carve-cli \- the operations of git carve as commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git-carve-cli
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every operation of the
.BR git-carve (1)
terminal UI as a command, the interface its test suite drives. It is
internal and not kept stable, and
.B make install
leaves it out
.RB ( "make install\-cli"
installs it). The exit status is 0 on success, and otherwise non-zero
with a one-line message on standard error. An operation that stops on a
conflict also exits non-zero.
.SH SELECTIONS
Line operations take a selection naming lines by the numbers
.B git-carve-cli diff
(or
.BR "git-carve-cli show" )
prints:
.TP
.BI n N
New-side line
.I N
(an added line).
.TP
.BI o N
Old-side line
.I N
(a removed line).
.TP
.BI n A \- B ", o" A \- B
A range takes every changed line whose number lies in it. A removed
line counts as sitting at the number of the new line after it, and for
.B o
an added line at the old line before it. A range then takes both
halves of a changed line.
.PP
Items are separated by commas, for example
.B o12,n12
(the old and new version of line 12) or
.BR n250-260,n300 .
.B new:
and
.B old:
are long forms of the prefixes
.RB ( old:12,new:12 ).
No selection starts with a dash, and none is mistaken for an option.
Options may appear anywhere, and
.B \-\-
ends them for a path that starts with a dash.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR diff " [" \-\-cached "] [" \-U \fIn\fR "] [" \-W "] [" \-w "] \fIpath\fR"
Annotated unstaged (or staged) diff with old and new line numbers.
Untracked files are shown as new files.
The options set the context lines, show whole functions and ignore
whitespace, as for
.BR "git diff" .
The line numbers stay valid for the other commands.
.TP
.BI "stage " "path sel" ", unstage " "path sel"
Stage or unstage the selected lines. Unstage uses the numbers of
.BR "diff \-\-cached" .
.TP
.BI "stage-file " path... ", unstage-file " path...
Whole files.
.TP
.B renames
List staged renames and renames in the work tree, with their
similarity.
.TP
.BI "stage-move " "from to" ", unstage-move " "from to"
Stage only the move of a rename, or unstage both halves of a rename.
Line numbers of a renamed file (in
.BR diff ,
.BR stage ,
.BR unstage )
refer to its diff against the old file, before and after the move is
staged.
.TP
.BR set-staged " [" \-\-cached "] \fIpath sel file\fR"
Replace the selected region of the index entry with the content of
.I file
(\fB\-\fR for stdin). Without
.B \-\-cached
the region is taken from the unstaged diff as it will look once the
selection is staged. With it the region comes from the staged diff as
it is. This stages content that never existed on disk, such as an
intermediate version of a call between a rename and a signature change.
.TP
.BR "set-staged \-\-print" " [" \-\-cached "] \fIpath sel\fR"
Print that region.
.TP
.BR commit " [" \-m " \fImsg\fR | " \-F " \fIfile\fR | " \-\-draft "] [" \-\-amend "] [" \-s " | " \-\-no\-signoff ]
Commit what is staged.
.B \-\-draft
commits with the message being written (see
.BR message )
and then empties it. During a split the editor starts from a
template (see SPLITTING).
Without
.B \-m
or
.BR \-F ,
the editor starts with the subsystem prefix the history of the staged
files uses most, such as
.BR "net: phy: " .
The later commits of a split do the same.
.B carve.suggestPrefix
set to false turns that off.
.B \-s
adds the committer's
.B Signed-off-by
trailer unless that exact trailer is already in the message. git's own
.B \-s
only looks at the last trailer and would double a sign-off before a
.BR Change-Id .
This holds for amends and split templates too.
.TP
.BR message " [" \-\-set " \fIfile\fR | " \-\-clear " | " \-\-template ]
The commit message being written, the TUI's Message pane
.RB ( $GIT_DIR/CARVE_MESSAGE ):
print it, set it from a file (- for stdin), clear it, or print what an
empty one starts from (the editor template of the next commit).
.TP
.BR split " [\fIcommit\fR]"
Split HEAD or an older commit. Its changes become unstaged, to be
staged and committed piece by piece. Merges, dirty trees and operations
in progress are refused. Splitting a commit that the upstream already
contains only warns.
.TP
.B split \-\-finish
Allowed when nothing is left to stage and the new commits add up to the
original commit's tree exactly, or to that tree without the lines
discarded (
.BR d ,
.BR discard )
during the split: those are taken out of the commit on purpose. A file
edited during the split cannot give lines away that way (commit or
restore the edits first), and splitting an older commit does not allow
it. For an older commit, the commits above it are replayed and must keep
their trees.
.TP
.BR "split \-\-abort" " [" \-\-force ]
Restore the original commit, index and work tree exactly. Refused if
the work tree has changes that are not part of the original commit,
unless
.BR \-\-force .
Lines discarded during the split come back.
If the split was started where a rebase of yours stopped and that rebase
has moved on since (continued or aborted), resetting to the commit would
throw its steps away: abort refuses, and
.B \-\-force
only forgets the split and changes nothing else.
.TP
.B split \-\-status
Show the split in progress.
.TP
.BI "show " commit " \fR[\fIpath\fR]"
Annotated diff of one commit's changes to
.I path
(for
.BR defer ).
Without a path it lists the files the commit changes, one per line with a
status letter
.RB ( A ,
.BR M ,
.BR D ,
.BR T ,
and
.B R
with the old and the new path for a rename, whose diff is between the
old and the new content), as the Commit section lists them.
.TP
.BI "fixup " commit ", fixup \-\-amend " "\fR[\fB\-m \fImsg\fR | \fB\-F \fIfile\fR] " commit
A commit to be squashed into
.I commit
later, as
.B git commit \-\-fixup
makes it:
.B fixup! <subject>
holding what is staged (refused with nothing staged), or with
.BR \-\-amend ,
.B amend! <subject>
followed by the new message of
.I commit
(given, read from
.IR file ,
or in the editor starting from its current message), holding what is
staged if anything. The amend! commit is written by git carve, the same
as git 2.32's
.B \-\-fixup=amend:
makes it. Nothing is rewritten.
.TP
.BI "squash " "\fR[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fIbase\fR]"
Squash the
.BR fixup! ,
.B amend!
and
.B squash!
commits of the series
.RI ( base ..HEAD,
by default @{upstream}..HEAD, without an upstream the last 200 commits)
into their commits, as
.B git rebase \-\-autosquash
does. Each goes right after the commit its subject names, by subject, by
an id prefix or by the start of a subject, with repeated prefixes read
through. A fixup keeps the commit's message, an amend! replaces it with
its own on every git version, and a squash! adds its own to it.
It lists each fixup and its commit first, and
.B \-\-dry\-run
stops there. A fixup whose commit is not in the series stays where it
is. The rebase starts at the oldest commit changed. The series ends in
the same tree as before, else nothing is changed. Staged and unstaged
changes stay as they are. A conflict stops. Resolve it, stage, and run
.B git-carve-cli continue
or
.B abort
to put everything back. Merges in the way are refused.
.TP
.BI "fixup-to " "commit path sel" ", fixup-to \-\-cached " commit
Move the selected unstaged lines (or everything staged) into an older
commit. Commits above it that touch the same lines stop with a
conflict. Resolve, stage and run
.BR "git-carve-cli continue" ,
or
.BR "git-carve-cli abort" .
Your other work tree changes are kept exactly as they are. If the
change touches lines that the target does not have (a later commit
added them), nothing is changed and the command fails.
Files renamed since the target are followed: the change lands in the
file under its old name. A rename that is moved always takes both
halves.
While splitting HEAD, before the first commit of the split,
.B fixup\-to HEAD
moves a part of the split commit into the commit below it, such as a
change in patch 3 that belongs in patch 2. Older targets wait until the
split is finished.
.TP
.BR defer " \fIcommit path sel\fR [" \-\-after " \fIcommit\fR] [" \-m " \fImsg\fR]"
Move the selected lines of an older commit's diff out of it into a new
commit right after it (or after a later commit). The new commit's tree
equals the original commit's, and commits above keep their trees.
.TP
.BR gc " [" \-\-force ]
Remove what git carve left behind and is provably dead. That is state files
with no operation in progress, temporary indexes and worktrees whose
process is gone, undo entries beyond
.BR carve.undoLimit ,
and refs git carve no longer uses. It also folds the undo steps of an
operation that ended outside git carve.
A work-tree snapshot or parked changes left behind may be the only copy
of someone's work. They are listed with how to get them back and are
never removed. Without
.B \-\-force
it is a dry run.
Everything git carve keeps is in refs, and
.BR "git gc" ,
.B git clean
and
.B git worktree prune
never take it, also in the middle of an operation.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.TP
.B prefix
Print the subsystem prefix the history of the staged files uses most.
.TP
.BR blame " [" \-\-cached "] \fIpath\fR [\fIsel\fR]"
The commits that last touched the lines of the change to
.I path
(the selected lines, else all, and the staged ones with
.BR \-\-cached ),
as the commit list's
.B b
finds them: one line each, most lines first, as
.IR lines / blamed " " "id subject" .
.TP
.BR absorb " [" \-\-apply "] [\fIrange\fR]"
For every unstaged change block, find the commit of the series
(default
.BR @{upstream}..HEAD )
that last touched its lines. It runs
.B "git blame \-M"
once per file, with
.B \-C
too when
.B carve.absorbCopies
is set, and for an insertion it looks at the lines around it. With
.B \-\-apply
it moves each block there like
.BR fixup-to ,
with the same result checks. Blocks whose lines come from several
commits or from outside the series stay and are listed, and whole-file
changes stay too. Without
.B \-\-apply
it only prints the plan. Nothing may be staged. If a move stops on a
conflict, resolve it and continue, then absorb again for the rest.
.TP
.BR continue ", " abort " [" \-\-force "], " status
Continue, abort or show the operation in progress.
.TP
.BR resolve " " \-\-ours " | " \-\-theirs " \fIpath\fR [\fIn\fR]"
Take one side of conflict
.I n
(counted from 1 in the file), or of all conflicts of the file
(git checkout \-\-ours / \-\-theirs). The file is staged once no conflict
marker is left in it.
.B git checkout \-m
.I path
brings the conflicts back.
.TP
.BR substitute " \fIpath sel\fR s/\fIpattern\fR/\fIreplacement\fR/[gi]" ,
.BR "substitute \-\-all" " \fIpath\fR s/.../"
Replace in the work-tree lines
.I sel
(new-side numbers, the work tree's own, and old-side ones are refused),
or in the whole file, as the TUI's
.B :s
does. Line ends are kept and the index is not touched. Undo puts the
file back.
.TP
.BR ignore " \fIpath\fR..."
Add untracked files to
.BR .git/info/exclude ,
each matched exactly from the top. git then no longer lists them.
Tracked files are refused.
.TP
.BR remove " \fIpath\fR..."
.BR "git rm" :
delete the files from the work tree and stage their deletion, local
changes included (undo brings them back). Untracked files are refused
.RB ( discard\-file
deletes them), and a split in progress is refused too.
.TP
.BR discard " \fIpath sel\fR"
Throw the selected unstaged lines away from the work tree. The index
is not touched.
.TP
.BR discard-file " \fIpath\fR..."
Throw all unstaged changes of the files away by checking out the index
version. An untracked or intent-to-add file is deleted. Both are
recorded for
.BR undo ,
and undo puts the exact bytes and mode back.
.TP
.BR undo " [" \-\-force ]
Undo the last change to the index or HEAD (see
.BR UNDO ).
.TP
.B undo \-\-list
What can be undone, newest first.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-carve (1)
